{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/promethean-the-created-aa/4560-suggested-reading","id":4560,"title":"Suggested Reading","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Promethean: The Created"],"posts":[{"date":"2013-11-06T13:23:52-08:00","id":4560,"title":"Suggested Reading","content":"I'm in the planning stages of a new Promethean chronicle. It will be taking place some months from now but I want to do some reading to get an idea for the themes of the game. Does anyone know a relatively simple and accessable book on the basics of philosophy?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/785-full-time-gm","name":"Full Time GM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar785_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"174"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T13:25:04-08:00","id":4562,"title":"","content":"The core book is all you need the other books in the line do help but are optional. You don't need them but they do help. Raven or Data can help you more on what books do what.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T13:57:25-08:00","id":4573,"title":"","content":"The core book is very nice; each of the Refinements is presented in more detail than their parrallels in other gamelines for example. I've ordered a copy of Strange Alchemies because it seems to expand on the existing information. I'm not familiar with Raven or Data. Are they search engines?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/785-full-time-gm","name":"Full Time GM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar785_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"174"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T14:06:07-08:00","id":4579,"title":"","content":"Nah, they're posters. They know way more about Promethean then I do. I've read a few books but the setting doesn't jive with me so I can't really remember them. If you just want to know what powers, merits, or other benefits prometheans get without detail I would suggest you go to here <a href=\"http://wodindex.wikispaces.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://wodindex.wikispaces.com/</a> It's no substitute to an actual book but if you want to know what powers are where this helps.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T14:19:36-08:00","id":4587,"title":"","content":"I don't need the mechanics of the game; I'd like to try and arrange allegory and metaphor in the chronicle for each of the players and I want to use real world philosophy concepts. The idea is to make the players think differently, via their characters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/785-full-time-gm","name":"Full Time GM","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar785_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"174"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T15:23:21-08:00","id":4643,"title":"","content":"<b>Strange Alchemies</b> and <b>Magnum Opus</b> are generally the most useful sourcebooks, expanding on the Refinements, adding new ones and weird characcter creation options (like clay Golems or stone Muses). <b>Saturnine Night</b> is essential if you want to play as one of the Zeka (Nuclear Prometheans) or Unflesh (robot/machine Prometheans), <b>Pandora's Book</b> is all about Pandorans, Centimani and Qashmallim.<br /> <br /> But you're interested more in suggested reading of a more philosophical bent, so... I guess Camus, Sartre and Nietzsche are good, if obvious, choices for their work on nihilism, solipsism, existentialism, existential angst, determinism and such. Kierkegaard might also be useful on that front. Maybe some Descartes for Dualism, actually. I'm not a philosopher myself but I have far too many friends who've studied it at A-level or degree level, and a lot of it rubs off.<br /> <br /> You should also consider religious texts. As I've said before,echoes of every religion or spiritual belief can be found in Promethean. In fact, as we lost it with the forum move, I'll quote myself here...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">Of course, Promethean has a </span></span><i>lot </i><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">of religious, mainly Abrahamic, themes. The two main choirs of </span></span><i>qashmal </i><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">roughly correspond to angels and demons. The Demiurges have all done something that they really, really shouldn't have done (and suffer for it). A Promethean's Pilgrimage is pretty damn religious, especially when angels pop up to give them information/quests and the like. The very earth rejects them as unholy, untouched by divinity, a walking blasphemy.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">But let's take a wider look...</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">The Jewish focus on the covenant between G-d and a chosen people rings true for Prometheans. The Pilgrimage can also be seen as a search for enlightenment and escape from suffering (with some </span></span><i>very literal </i><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">reincarnation) in the vein of Buddhism. Overcoming ignorance to achieve a state of Nirvana, with karma and consequences for your actions (with monastic life encouraged)... Easy to see how that fits. You can easily couch it in the terms of animism (look at the Ulgan) and cast the</span></span><i>qashmal </i><span style=\"color:#404040\"><span style=\"font-family:Verdana\">as spirits, or in polytheistic terms (it does draw a lot from Greek and Egyptian mythology as well). Dualistic beliefs (like Zoroastrianism, if we simplify it. Interestingly, the faithful consider fire and water holy and often worship in the presence of fire) can easily see the reflections with the Elpis/Flux, Promethean/Pandoran and Elpidos/Lilithim breakdown, and other faiths focus more on tenets of cosmic balance can also be represented in this model - the qi of Taoism/Daoism could easily be Pyros (and Taoism is big on transformations) and the classic yin/yang breakdown is a Taoist idea. In the Baha'i faith mankind is seen as undergoing a constant spiritual evolution with a series of messengers taking us a step further each time. Remind you of anything? </span></span> </div> </div> </div>An odd suggestion is a computer game - Planescape: Torment. It's one of the most philosophical games ever made and the protagonist is, in many ways, not unlike a Promethean himself...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>Onyx Path Freelancer: Demon Storyteller's Guide, Dark Eras, Night Horrors: Enemy Action</i><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/825063-storyteller-s-guide-extra-material\" target=\"_blank\">Storyteller's Guide Extra Material</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/899423-after-the-fall-bonus-material\" target=\"_blank\">After the Fall: Bonus Material</a>","edited":{"userid":452,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","user":"Eremite","date":". 11-06-2013, 06:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/452-eremite","name":"Eremite","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar452_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-06T15:25:45-08:00","id":4648,"title":"","content":"Some of the themes are Kafkaesque (Huh this is actually a word) as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/116-epimetheus","name":"Epimetheus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1758"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-11T06:13:57-08:00","id":8005,"title":"","content":"I found Kirkegaard incredibly preachy, personally. But that's not really relevant.<br /> <br /> It sounds patronising, but there's a series of books, &quot;50 X ideas you really need to know&quot;. There's one for <a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/Philosophy-Ideas-Really-Need-Know/dp/1847240062\" target=\"_blank\">philosophy generally</a>, hits most of the major ideas in an easily accessible way if you're not used to philosophy. I'd actually recommend steering clear of the so-called 'major' texts if you're new to them, simply on the basis that they <i>tend</i> to be pretty hard to grasp, and it sounds like you're mostly reading for a game. Even if you're curious, I'd still recommend starting with easier ones, most bookshops have one or two geared at that kind of level. What a lot of people don't understand <span style=\"font-family:arial\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\">is philosophy is like reading any other field: you need to get your feet under you with the basic stuff first. I mean, you wouldn't start learning about physics by reading Einsteins &quot;On the Quantum Mechanics of Radiation&quot;.<br /> <br /> I'd also advise caution with putting too much philosophy in your story. I mean, a bit is fine, can really make things interesting, but too much gets annoying quick. A little goes a long way. Also, don't expect your players to necessarily even notice it, or care if they do. That's how how it goes (I don't know if you've run before). You know your group best, of course, these are just general concerns.<br /> <br /> Religions are a good suggestion, they tend to take a viewpoint that Western society (which tends to be either Christian-ish or secular in tone, depending on exactly where you live and your individual experience and interpretation) doesn't really consider often. Buddhism is a really interesting one, also voudon (aka voodoo, although that's technically different). They might shake some ideas loose.</span></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1mSW2-p-SwmYw5gaqnVMQ7HcRNhVedC-aA4vB6sBclGk/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for GMs</a> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1hslzrmui6ML-v7LniQUXLNyy8o52YF9g2u6usdu-Drc/edit?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">My Commandments for Players</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/207-allan53","name":"Allan53","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"966"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2013-11-13T08:08:14-08:00","id":9696,"title":"","content":"There's also the <a href=\"http://www.amazon.co.uk/s/ref=nb_sb_ss_i_0_16/280-0861330-7912068?url=search-alias%3Dstripbooks&amp;field-keywords=pop%20culture%20and%20philosophy&amp;sprefix=pop+culture+and+%2Cstripbooks%2C288&amp;rh=i%3Astripbooks%2Ck%3Apop%20culture%20and%20philosophy\" target=\"_blank\">Pop Culture and Philosophy</a> series which are great about giving a short run-down on the &quot;greatest hits&quot; of philosophy by relating them to pop culture figures. Lots of discussion about how to be a person, morals, &quot;What Would Tony Stark Do?&quot;, etc.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>- If you must be ridiculous, I must ridicule you.<br /> - Those that can give up essential liberties in exchange for temporary safety deserve neither liberty nor safety. -- Benjamin Franklin</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/375-papa-bear","name":"Papa Bear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar375_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1920"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}